WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:06.260
TAD HUNT: Hi, everybody.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.052
Today, we're going to
talk about optimizing

00:00:08.052 --> 00:00:09.670
file storage for your use case.

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.930
I'm Tad Hunt, and I manage
the Filestore portfolio

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:13.095
on Google Cloud.

00:00:13.095 --> 00:00:14.470
I have a few goals
for this talk.

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:16.090
First, you walk away
with an understanding

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:18.298
that Google Cloud is ready
for your applications that

00:00:18.298 --> 00:00:19.480
need file storage.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:22.053
Second, if you're migrating
File workloads to Cloud,

00:00:22.053 --> 00:00:23.470
you understand
that you don't have

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:26.590
to re-architect for
alternative storage solutions.

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:29.530
Correspondingly, if you are
running workloads in Cloud that

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:31.780
might benefit from
file, you now know

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:33.010
how to take advantage of it.

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:34.660
And finally, this
knowledge enables

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:38.460
you to simplify and accelerate
your public cloud [INAUDIBLE]..

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.678
To set some early context
so you can get the most out

00:00:40.678 --> 00:00:42.720
of your time, we'll start
with an overview of why

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.557
File is important, then
deep dive into Filestore

00:00:45.557 --> 00:00:47.140
and a couple of key
file partnerships,

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:50.090
and then we'll wrap it up
by tying it all together.

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:52.670
So first, the file landscape.

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.380
So let's start by
answering the question,

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:55.610
where do I store my data?

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.190
In a taxonomy of
storage systems,

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.335
we generally break it
down into structured,

00:00:59.335 --> 00:01:02.980
i.e. databases, and unstructured
storage systems, generally file

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:04.420
block and object.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:06.910
File storage is generally
focused on unstructured data

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:08.980
storage problems and
is critically important

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.020
for enterprise
applications like SAP,

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:14.562
infrastructure like VMware, and
all sorts of things in between.

00:01:14.562 --> 00:01:16.270
Since this is an
intermediate-level talk,

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:18.603
I'm going to assume you're
somewhat familiar with shared

00:01:18.603 --> 00:01:20.980
file storage, commonly known
as NAS, or Network-Attached

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:21.712
Storage.

00:01:21.712 --> 00:01:23.170
If you've made it
this far, chances

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:24.837
are that you have a
file storage problem

00:01:24.837 --> 00:01:26.680
that you need to
solve in GCP, so we

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:29.020
will be focusing on
file capabilities, use

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.730
cases, and workloads.

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:32.890
It all starts here.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:35.590
Shared file systems
are the arteries

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.293
of enterprise applications.

00:01:37.293 --> 00:01:38.710
When the public
cloud was born, it

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:40.440
didn't have traditional
file services.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.088
It had brand-new, cool
stuff like object storage,

00:01:43.088 --> 00:01:45.130
which enabled brand new
use cases and application

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:46.120
architectures.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.100
But lack of shared
file services meant

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.650
that the cloud just was not
ready for most enterprise

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:51.700
applications.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:53.680
Today, Google Cloud is ready.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:57.160
We offer a mature set of file
storage services, both natively

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:00.310
and from industry-leading
partners.

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:02.050
Look, we all know
that file workloads

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:03.070
are incredibly varied.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:05.710
And as a consequence, there's
not a single file storage

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:07.480
solution for all use cases.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.539
This view you see here is
one way to slice and dice it.

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:12.850
Sometimes, GCP's
offering, Filestore,

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:15.130
will be the best choice,
and sometimes, it'll

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:16.252
be a partner offering.

00:02:16.252 --> 00:02:17.710
For these reasons,
we are committed

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:19.840
to supporting a strong
partner ecosystem.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:21.820
Eliminating friction
from cloud migrations

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:24.070
often means providing the
same tools in the cloud

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:26.320
as customers are using
on-premise, usually

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.930
due to the advanced features
their applications depend on.

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:30.370
Our partners
complement Filestore

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:33.520
with things like extreme scale
app capacity and performance,

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.210
rich and deep feature sets with
long histories baked deeply

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:38.980
into enterprise infrastructure,
support for hybrid cloud

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:40.750
and container
storage, the ability

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:42.730
to stitch together
many distributed sites,

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:44.320
and of course,
there's many more.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:46.960
We believe that a capable
Google offering coupled

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.510
with a strong partner
ecosystem recruits the best

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:51.790
expertise across the
industry to solve

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:53.050
the problems you care about.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:56.197
The end result is you get the
best solution for your problem.

00:02:56.197 --> 00:02:58.030
And we can't hope to
cover everything today,

00:02:58.030 --> 00:02:59.740
so we're going to
focus on a few examples

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.980
where some of these
different solutions excel.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:04.300
So that's the landscape.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:07.490
Next, let's dive into
how Filestore fits.

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:09.740
Let's start with how one
of our Filestore customers

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.850
is leveraging Filestore
to enable their business.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:16.070
Filestore has been generally
available since April 2019,

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:18.530
and Flywheel has been
along for the ride.

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:20.990
They're huge fans because their
WordPress hosting business

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:23.750
depends on reliable and
performant file storage.

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:26.238
Filestore provides that,
while simultaneously enabling

00:03:26.238 --> 00:03:28.280
Flywheel to get out of
the storage infrastructure

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:31.470
management business.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.300
What is Filestore good at?

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:34.810
So more than this, of course.

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:36.477
But some of the key
workloads that we've

00:03:36.477 --> 00:03:38.760
already tuned Filestore
are media rendering,

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.440
application migrations,
web content management,

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.370
which is what Flywheel is
doing, media processing,

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:48.130
and software
development and testing.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:50.460
So how do we help
customers like Flywheel,

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:53.578
or for that matter, you,
focus on your business?

00:03:53.578 --> 00:03:55.370
We make it easy to pick
the right Filestore

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:58.010
tier for your workload
by offering two flavors.

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:00.510
First, we have Filestore Basic,
which is generally available

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:03.330
and has been since
April 2019, and we just

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:06.030
launched Filestore
High Scale to beta

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:08.790
in June, so just
not too long ago

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:11.740
if you're watching this in July.

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:14.387
So Filestore Basic is optimized
for enterprise workloads that

00:04:14.387 --> 00:04:16.470
don't have large capacity
and performance demands,

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.350
but they do require
enterprise-class reliability,

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:21.329
whereas High Scale is
optimized for use when

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:23.400
performance and
scalability are really

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.433
critical to your applications.

00:04:25.433 --> 00:04:26.850
You may be aware
that last August,

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:28.860
we acquired a company
called Elastifile.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:30.780
High Scale is our
first Filestore product

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.850
built by the integrated
team, and it's based

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:34.170
on the Elastifile file system.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:38.750
So let's talk for a
minute about performance.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:41.240
Filestore offers
great latency, IOPS,

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:43.050
and throughput--
as I like to say,

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:44.820
the trifecta of
storage performance.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.330
Equally as important, not only
do you get great performance,

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:49.105
but you get consistent
performance, too,

00:04:49.105 --> 00:04:52.000
and this enables predictability
for your applications.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.750
We offer three Filestore
performance tiers

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:56.850
to optimize for workloads with
different performance needs.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:00.050
For example, if your workload
is a low-traffic enterprise app,

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:01.700
say a web app with
low usage, you

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:06.282
might just deploy a
Filestore Basic HDD instance.

00:05:06.282 --> 00:05:08.240
On the other hand, maybe
you have a render farm

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.970
built on preemptible VMs
with thousands of nodes

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:12.350
and 10 thousands of cores.

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:15.050
You might pick High Scale to
minimize your render times.

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:16.970
Or maybe your app is
somewhere between.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:20.233
Maybe you're an intense random
read or random write workload,

00:05:20.233 --> 00:05:21.650
but you've only
got a few clients.

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:24.830
Maybe you'd like a Monte Carlo
simulation for a single user.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:28.940
Maybe that is the right signal
to pick Filestore Basic SSD,

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:32.270
in order to get the
maximum single-client IOPS.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.260
So we didn't just
make Filestore fast,

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:36.260
we also made it simple
to reason about the price

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:38.090
and the performance
that you get.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:40.960
When performance and
capacity are the key needs,

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:42.890
Filestore High
Scale fits the bill.

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:45.620
For those of you with advanced
file system knowledge,

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:47.830
Filestore Basic is a
scale-up architecture,

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:50.690
while Filestore High Scale
is a scale-out architecture.

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:52.610
What this means is that
High Scale performance

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:55.550
scales linearly with
capacity and it can handle

00:05:55.550 --> 00:05:57.330
an enormous number of clients.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:59.330
With all of our
Filestore tiers, you just

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:01.147
pick a tier and a
size at creation time

00:06:01.147 --> 00:06:03.230
so that you know immediately
what your performance

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:04.770
characteristics are.

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:06.797
And since we don't charge
per-operation fees,

00:06:06.797 --> 00:06:08.880
you also know immediately
what your spend will be,

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:12.060
and we even show you an
estimate in the console.

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:15.120
So Harvard selected the
Elastifile-based Google

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:17.550
Filestore for its extremely
powerful performance,

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:20.912
customization capabilities,
and simplicity of use.

00:06:20.912 --> 00:06:23.370
This is an example of where
Filestore High Scale has helped

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:25.560
Harvard with COVID-19 research.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:27.150
They've been doing
virtual screenings

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.310
of potential COVID-19
treatments and they've

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:31.840
found some promising candidates
through this research.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:33.382
We've got a really
extensive write-up

00:06:33.382 --> 00:06:35.700
about this on our launch
blog that's linked here.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:37.950
This is a good example of
a batch compute workload

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:40.080
where Filestore's
scale-out performance

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:43.790
helps with high throughput
computing workloads.

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:46.315
Which is kind of a good
segue into our tech specs,

00:06:46.315 --> 00:06:48.440
since we're getting a little
bit technical, anyway.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:52.492
First, I'm going
to cover a gestalt

00:06:52.492 --> 00:06:53.700
of what I'm showing you here.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.480
I'm not going to just
read the slide to you.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:59.030
So first, Filestore
is a zonal service.

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:02.960
It offers an SLA of three nines
for the basic tiers, which

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:07.710
when High Scale launches to GA,
we'll also match in High Scale.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:09.920
With Filestore Basic,
you can go up to 64

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:14.230
T. In High Scale, that
extends that up to 320 T.

00:07:14.230 --> 00:07:15.980
You don't have to start
with a large filer

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:18.830
because for all of these
variants of Filestore,

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:23.162
you can actually grow online
with no downtime, on demand.

00:07:23.162 --> 00:07:24.620
For protocol support,
we've got you

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:27.980
covered with NFS, which
is kind of the Linux

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:30.710
industry-leading protocol.

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:32.780
We also include file
locking, and this

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:34.790
is true across all tiers.

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:37.250
I would say generally,
NFS can sometimes

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:39.800
work for Microsoft
Windows workloads,

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:42.380
but most of the time, we would
recommend a partner offering,

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:44.005
and I'll talk about
that a little later

00:07:44.005 --> 00:07:45.830
if you have a Windows workload.

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:49.190
This is a Google product, so
you get Google-grade encryption

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:54.380
and security, including
encryption at rest.

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:57.600
We also recently rolled out beat
support for per-IP range NFS

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:58.760
security controls.

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:02.543
If you're an NFS expert,
what I'm about to say

00:08:02.543 --> 00:08:03.710
would mean something to you.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:05.930
Otherwise, you'll be like, eh?

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:08.510
So anyway, NFS has
the ability to set

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:10.010
this thing called root squash.

00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:12.190
And of course, read-only
export modes, I think,

00:08:12.190 --> 00:08:13.570
is pretty self-explanatory.

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:16.250
Root squash is a user ID mapping
so that you can make sure

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:19.190
that root on the
client is not allowed

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:23.460
to access the file system
and do some remapping there.

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:25.210
We're also integrated
with Stackdriver,

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:26.668
which enables you
to set up alerts,

00:08:26.668 --> 00:08:28.450
for example, if free
space gets too low,

00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:30.480
or if you deleted
a bunch of data

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:33.150
and you realize you're
wasting a lot of space.

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:36.510
And also, when you have
application performance issues,

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:38.262
we have a whole
host of biometrics

00:08:38.262 --> 00:08:39.970
that you can look at
to help you identify

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:41.678
why your applications
might be performing

00:08:41.678 --> 00:08:44.039
as well as they should be.

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:47.005
Finally, there's three ways to
configure and manage Filestore.

00:08:47.005 --> 00:08:48.630
You can do it through
the command line,

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:52.020
through the standard
Google Cloud API,

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:54.540
and of course, we've
got the web console.

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:56.070
This is obviously
a brief overview

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:59.340
of what Filestore offers, so
check out our docs at the link

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:02.160
here for more details.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:04.290
Moving on from the
core file features,

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:07.640
I want to talk about
some of the integrations.

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:11.270
Some of the highlights here
are for Compute Engine,

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:13.640
we fully support
preemptible VMs,

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:15.400
which are commonly
used to optimize cost

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:16.610
for batch workloads.

00:09:16.610 --> 00:09:20.522
In Kubernetes Engine, you can
use Filestore automatically

00:09:20.522 --> 00:09:22.730
and configure it automatically
through the native NFS

00:09:22.730 --> 00:09:26.300
plugins through the
CSI interface, as well.

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:27.890
On the networking
side, Filestore

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:30.050
is peered with your
VPC network, and that

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:32.780
enables you to access it
from authorized clients

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:34.460
on that network.

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:36.830
And then based on
the IP access control

00:09:36.830 --> 00:09:39.180
feature I mentioned
on the prior slide,

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:42.650
you can further restrict
which of those VMs on your VPC

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:44.100
are allowed to access it.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:45.770
We talked about
Stackdriver already.

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:48.470
And then last, but not
least, for the config-as-code

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:52.210
and the
infrastructure-as-code models

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:54.830
that many enterprises
are using today,

00:09:54.830 --> 00:09:59.790
we're integrated with Deployment
Manager and Terraform.

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:05.580
So moving on, so let's say that
your workload fits into one

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:07.597
of the Filestore tiers.

00:10:07.597 --> 00:10:08.430
You've decided that.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:09.347
You've checked it out.

00:10:09.347 --> 00:10:10.680
It seems to fit the bill.

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:14.530
One of the next things
on your mind is probably,

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:16.780
where do I have to put my
app to take advantage of it?

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:19.290
For Filestore Basic,
the answer is anywhere

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:22.710
Google Cloud has a region,
you can use it there.

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:24.150
We're in all the regions.

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:25.710
For High Scale,
we just launched,

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:29.620
and we're already in one region
in each of the US, European,

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:30.860
and Asian markets.

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:35.160
And as the product
matures, we'll of course

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:36.565
show up in more places.

00:10:36.565 --> 00:10:38.940
I should note that this is a
point-in-time view from July

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:39.810
2020.

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:41.950
If you're watching
this in the far future

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:44.460
and you need High Scale in
a region not listed here,

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:45.630
check out the docs.

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:47.910
Chances are, things
have changed.

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:50.250
At any point in
time, if you do think

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:52.680
that High Scale is what you
need and it's not available

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:54.300
and you need it, please just
reach out to your account

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:55.650
manager to discuss options.

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:58.290
They can get in touch
with me and my team

00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:02.020
and we can probably hook you up.

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:03.770
I'm going to wrap up
the Filestore section

00:11:03.770 --> 00:11:06.080
by just briefly covering
a couple of backup partner

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:07.497
integrations, and
then we're going

00:11:07.497 --> 00:11:09.560
to move on to other file
partner integrations.

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:12.260
The first is Actifio Sky,
which provides application

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:13.836
consistent backup and recovery.

00:11:13.836 --> 00:11:15.586
And here's some
information and some links

00:11:15.586 --> 00:11:17.620
for you to check out more.

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:20.660
And then we'll move
on to Commvault,

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:23.780
which is designed to protect
data residing in Filestore

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:25.120
and many other.

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:28.790
Commvault and Actifio are
both very well-known partner

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:31.720
offerings for backups.

00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:33.793
Commvault enables efficient
and secure backups

00:11:33.793 --> 00:11:35.960
via their compression,
deduplication, and encryption

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:39.870
engines, and they enable
recovery to any Cloud Filestore

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:43.610
instance, or you can even
restore your data directly

00:11:43.610 --> 00:11:46.064
to any Windows or
Linux computers.

00:11:46.064 --> 00:11:49.220
And that about wraps
it up for Filestore.

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:52.292
Before we move on to talk
about our file partnerships,

00:11:52.292 --> 00:11:54.500
the links here will help
jump help you jump in to get

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:56.083
started with
provisioning and managing

00:11:56.083 --> 00:11:59.350
any of your Filestore instances.

00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:02.020
So moving on to file partners,
as I mentioned in the landscape

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:03.730
section, we integrate
with partners

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:04.990
for a wide range of use cases.

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:07.570
And that means we work with
way too many partners for me

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:11.050
to cover in one overview video.

00:12:11.050 --> 00:12:13.975
For this talk, I'll focus
on Dell EMC and NetApp

00:12:13.975 --> 00:12:16.100
because these are the two
partners we've integrated

00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:17.920
most deeply into the platform.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:19.720
So for example, they're
integrated directly

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:21.887
into our Cloud Console for
provisioning and managing

00:12:21.887 --> 00:12:23.217
of your file systems.

00:12:23.217 --> 00:12:24.800
They're integrated
into Cloud Billing,

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:27.510
so you get a single bill from
Google with everything on it.

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:29.260
And they're integrated
with Cloud Support,

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:30.850
so you don't have to
guess at who to reach out

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:31.870
to if you have an issue.

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:36.070
You just contact us, like
you would for anything else.

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:39.760
If your use case doesn't seem to
be covered by Dell or NetApp--

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:42.790
for example, you might have a
low-latency HPC workload that

00:12:42.790 --> 00:12:46.540
relies on Luster, or maybe you
have a branch office workload

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:49.390
where you've got 1,000
branch offices that all need

00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:52.550
access to the same file for
collaboration-- or anything

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:54.550
else that Filestore or
one of these two partners

00:12:54.550 --> 00:12:56.680
doesn't seem to be a good
fit for, please reach out

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:58.097
to us through your
account manager

00:12:58.097 --> 00:13:01.526
and like I mentioned before,
we'll help you get started

00:13:01.526 --> 00:13:04.480
with the right solution.

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:07.150
So let's jump in with
Dell's new offering.

00:13:07.150 --> 00:13:09.850
Previously, you might have
known this next product

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:12.280
I'm going to described
as Isilon or OneFS,

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:14.320
but Dell just went through
a rebranding exercise

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:17.230
to bring all of the EMC
product branding in line

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:19.000
with the rest of
the Dell ecosystem.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:21.280
And going forward, it's
now just called PowerScale.

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:23.780
Still with the same goodness
inside, just with the new name.

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:26.090
So PowerScale for
Google Cloud launch

00:13:26.090 --> 00:13:28.480
to general availability
just a couple of months ago,

00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:29.770
this past May.

00:13:29.770 --> 00:13:32.410
It provides an enterprise-class
native cloud experience

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:34.773
for enabling organizations
to accelerate innovation

00:13:34.773 --> 00:13:36.190
for high performance
applications,

00:13:36.190 --> 00:13:38.440
such as analytics, media
and entertainment, and life

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:39.670
sciences.

00:13:39.670 --> 00:13:42.840
PowerScale is built on the
mature Isilon OneFS file

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:44.440
system, and all of
its capabilities

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:46.372
are now available in GCP.

00:13:46.372 --> 00:13:48.580
Additionally, the fact that
this is a managed service

00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:50.650
brings some things you
don't get on-premises.

00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:53.650
Namely, you get an SLA,
experts from Google and Dell

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:56.070
who manage the service
so you don't have to,

00:13:56.070 --> 00:13:58.057
and you're billed
only for what you use.

00:13:58.057 --> 00:13:59.890
You don't have to buy
a whole bunch of space

00:13:59.890 --> 00:14:03.640
like you do on-prem, because
hard drives are large.

00:14:03.640 --> 00:14:05.800
For more information
or how to get started,

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:09.175
you can visit the link
here, or just search Google.

00:14:09.175 --> 00:14:11.988
It works pretty well, too.

00:14:11.988 --> 00:14:13.780
This is going to be a
really brief overview

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:15.470
of these couple of partners.

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:18.160
So next up, we're going
to dive into NetApp.

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:20.500
Dell has one really
big offering,

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:22.000
and NetApp really
has two offerings.

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:24.130
They have Cloud Volumes
Service, and we'll

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:26.305
talk about that, and
Cloud Volumes ONTAP.

00:14:26.305 --> 00:14:28.805
One is a managed service and
one is a self-managed solution.

00:14:28.805 --> 00:14:31.470
We're going to start with
Cloud Volumes Service.

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:34.420
Cloud Volumes Service has
been GA since October 2019,

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:37.152
and is a fully-managed
storage solution

00:14:37.152 --> 00:14:39.610
delivering high performance
and low latency for application

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:42.660
workloads, such as SAP
HANA, Google Cloud VMware

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:45.490
Engine, and Microsoft Windows.

00:14:45.490 --> 00:14:47.860
If you remember from before
when I said Filestore is not

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:50.680
great for Microsoft
Windows workloads,

00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:52.870
one of these partner
offerings is.

00:14:52.870 --> 00:14:56.100
They both have very, very
mature Microsoft Windows stacks.

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:57.635
That protocol is
called SMB, so they

00:14:57.635 --> 00:15:00.020
have very mature SMB stacks.

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:01.927
So NetApp Cloud
Volumes Service was

00:15:01.927 --> 00:15:03.760
designed with the
application owner in mind.

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:05.980
It looks and feels like a
native Google service, including

00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:08.020
the integrations into
the console, the billing,

00:15:08.020 --> 00:15:10.040
and support that I
mentioned before.

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:11.710
And then in addition
to that, it has

00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:13.600
three different
price-performance tiers,

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:16.030
as well as the ability to scale
performance dynamically up

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:19.023
and down with your application
and user requirements.

00:15:19.023 --> 00:15:20.440
This ensures that
your application

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:22.315
is right-sized through
its development, test,

00:15:22.315 --> 00:15:23.590
and production lifecycle.

00:15:23.590 --> 00:15:25.660
Additionally it
comes with an SLA

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:28.690
of three nines, which means it's
ready for your production use

00:15:28.690 --> 00:15:29.880
cases.

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:32.740
And then here's the link
to get more information

00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:35.070
or get started.

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:38.040
NetApp's second service is
called Cloud Volumes ONTAP.

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:40.350
So when your needs extend
beyond the capabilities

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:43.410
of Cloud Volumes Service, the
product I just talked about,

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:46.650
NetApp also offers Cloud Volumes
ONTAP as a self-managed storage

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:48.140
solution.

00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:50.960
So it delivers nearly the
entire set of storage management

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:53.090
and data optimization
capabilities

00:15:53.090 --> 00:15:57.782
available to on-premise
NetApp ONTAP users today.

00:15:57.782 --> 00:15:59.240
So if you're coming
from on-premise

00:15:59.240 --> 00:16:02.510
and you're already relying
on a lot of NetApp features,

00:16:02.510 --> 00:16:04.722
this might be a good
offering for you.

00:16:04.722 --> 00:16:06.680
It's really designed for
storage administrators

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:10.020
that are familiar with the
NetApp ONTAP operating system.

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:12.320
It provides a seamless method
to accelerate migration

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:14.720
of your on-premise
application workloads,

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:16.340
as well ensure your
on-premise data

00:16:16.340 --> 00:16:18.740
is protected safely in the
cloud for archive and disaster

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:20.900
recovery use cases.

00:16:20.900 --> 00:16:23.070
Again, for more information
or to get started,

00:16:23.070 --> 00:16:26.270
check out this link here.

00:16:26.270 --> 00:16:28.160
And that brings us to the end.

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:30.490
At this point, we're
going to wrap it up.

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:34.580
So going back to the intro,
tying it all together,

00:16:34.580 --> 00:16:36.440
shared file systems
are the arteries

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:38.240
of enterprise applications.

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:41.000
And everything we've covered
can be boiled down to the fact

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:43.397
that GCP is ready
for these workloads.

00:16:43.397 --> 00:16:45.230
I started with my goals
for this talk, which

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:46.938
are that you walk away
understanding that

00:16:46.938 --> 00:16:50.060
not only have Google Cloud's
file capabilities matured,

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:51.560
but now you have
some idea of how

00:16:51.560 --> 00:16:54.500
to leverage them to simplify
and accelerate your public cloud

00:16:54.500 --> 00:16:57.210
file storage [INAUDIBLE].

00:16:57.210 --> 00:16:57.900
Thank you.

00:16:57.900 --> 00:17:00.300
I hope this talk has
helped light the path.

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:02.210
Thanks for your time.

